---

<Base
  title="Monty Hall with N doors: does switching still win?"
  description="Move the door count and the number of doors the host opens, and watch the switching advantage collapse from 2x toward 1x. Exact probabilities plus a Monte Carlo simulation."
  category={CATEGORY}
  crumbTool={true}
>
  <nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
    <a href="/">Home</a> <span aria-hidden="true">›</span>
    <a href={cat.href}>{cat.name}</a> <span aria-hidden="true">›</span>
    Monty Hall with N doors
  </nav>

  <h1>Monty Hall with N doors: does switching still win?</h1>

  <p class="lead-kicker">September 1990</p>

  <p class="lead">
    A reader wrote to Marilyn vos Savant's column in <em>Parade</em>: three doors,
    one car, two goats. You pick a door; the host opens another to show a goat and
    offers the swap. She answered that switching wins two games in three, against
    one in three for staying. Then the mail came — thousands of letters, many from
    readers with doctorates, most telling her she had it wrong.
  </p>

  <p class="lead">
    Five years later Andrew Vazsonyi put the same problem to Paul Erdős, a
    houseguest at the time. By Vazsonyi's account Erdős rejected the answer, was
    unmoved by a decision tree, and gave in only after watching a simulation run —
    and still did not accept <em>why</em> until Ron Graham talked him through it
    days later.
  </p>

  <p class="lead">
    <span class="lead-turn">None of them settled it by arguing.</span> The
    disagreement was never about arithmetic — it was about a rule the question never
    pinned down, and about a number, the door count, that the model below does not
    put in charge. Move the sliders and watch which one does.
  </p>

  <div data-widget="monty-hall-n-doors"></div>

  <section class="math" aria-labelledby="math-title">
    <h2 class="math-title" id="math-title">The mathematics behind the sliders</h2>

    <p>
      The first pick is made with no information, and the host never opens the
      player's door. So the probability attached to that door is fixed at the
      moment it is chosen, and everything the host does afterwards redistributes
      the remaining probability among the doors the player did not pick.
    </p>

    <div class="math-eq">
      <span>P<sub>stay</sub></span><span class="op">=</span>
      <span class="frac"><span class="frac-num">1</span><span class="frac-den">N</span></span>
    </div>

    <div class="math-eq">
      <span>P<sub>switch</sub></span><span class="op">=</span>
      <span class="frac"><span class="frac-num">N − 1</span><span class="frac-den">N</span></span>
      <span class="op">×</span>
      <span class="frac"><span class="frac-num">1</span><span class="frac-den">R</span></span>
      <span class="op">=</span>
      <span class="frac"><span class="frac-num">N − 1</span><span class="frac-den">N (N − 1 − K)</span></span>
    </div>

    <dl class="math-where">
      <dt>N</dt><dd>door count, 3 to 100</dd>
      <dt>K</dt><dd>doors the host opens, 1 to N − 2 (never the player's door, never the prize)</dd>
      <dt>R</dt><dd>doors left to switch into, R = N − 1 − K</dd>
    </dl>

    <div class="math-steps-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Derivation steps">
    <table class="math-steps">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Step</th>
          <th scope="col">Expression</th>
          <th scope="col">Why</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>First pick holds the prize</td>
          <td>1 / N</td>
          <td>Uniform placement, no information yet</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>First pick is wrong</td>
          <td>(N − 1) / N</td>
          <td>Complement of row 1 — the prize is behind one of the N − 1 doors you did not pick</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Host removes K losing doors</td>
          <td>R = N − 1 − K</td>
          <td>Of those N − 1, the host opens K, all losing — so R stay closed</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Switch lands on the prize</td>
          <td>1 / R</td>
          <td>Given a wrong first pick, the prize is one of the R survivors</td>
        </tr>
        <tr class="math-result">
          <td>Switching wins</td>
          <td>(N − 1) / (N · R)</td>
          <td>Row 2 × row 4</td>
        </tr>
        <tr class="math-result">
          <td>Advantage over staying</td>
          <td>(N − 1) / R</td>
          <td>P<sub>switch</sub> ÷ P<sub>stay</sub> — doors not picked, over doors still closed</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    </div>

    <p class="math-check">
      <strong>Two checks.</strong> At N=3, K=1: R=1, so switching is 2/3 and the
      two probabilities sum to 1. Whenever the host opens every door but one
      (K = N−2, so R = 1) the same thing happens — the player faces exactly two
      doors, and switching is (N−1)/N. The advantage term (N−1)/R is the whole
      story: it compares the doors you did not pick against the doors still
      closed. Raise N while holding K fixed and both grow together, so the ratio
      slides toward 1 and the two probabilities converge.
    </p>

    <p>
      One property makes the formula usable: the result does not depend on
      <em>how</em> the host picks which losing doors to open. Under any rule the
      prize survives and R doors stay closed, so a random switch hits it with
      probability 1/R. These are unconditional probabilities — the whole
      population of games, not one game whose opened doors you have already seen.
    </p>
  </section>

  <h2>How to read what you are looking at</h2>

  <p>
    The chart has trials on the horizontal axis, on a logarithmic scale. The
    vertical axis is the share of games won, and it rescales to fit whatever the
    sliders are showing — at a hundred doors with one reveal both strategies land
    near 1%, and a fixed 0–100% axis would stack them on top of each other. So read
    the axis labels before comparing two settings; the window changes with the
    setting. The curve begins where the running average has settled inside that
    window, which the caption states.
  </p>

  <p>
    Each strategy gets two lines in the same colour: the <strong>solid line</strong>
    is the simulation's running average, the <strong>dashed line</strong> the exact
    probability from the formula. When the solid line converges onto its dashed
    partner, the simulation and the algebra agree.
  </p>

  <p>
    The table underneath ignores the simulation entirely. It lists exact
    probabilities for a fixed rule — the host opens every door except one — so you
    can compare door counts without dragging anything.
  </p>

  <h2>What this model turns over</h2>

  <p>
    The famous answer is that switching wins two games in three, against the
    intuition that two remaining doors must mean even odds. This model reproduces
    that at N=3, K=1: it puts staying at 33.3% and switching at 66.7%.
  </p>

  <p>
    Push both sliders to 100 doors and 98 reveals and the model separates the two
    strategies further — switching at 99.0% against 1.00% for staying. That is the
    version usually offered to make the result feel obvious.
  </p>

  <p>
    The reversal in this model appears when the two sliders move independently.
    Hold N at 100 and drag K back down to 1. The model now puts switching at 1.01%
    and staying at 1.00% — an advantage of 1.01×, down from 2.00× at three doors.
    A hundred doors, and in this model almost the whole benefit of switching is
    gone. It never reaches zero: for every legal setting the model keeps switching
    ahead, by a margin that shrinks toward nothing.
  </p>

  <p>
    So the model does not say that more doors favour switching. It puts the
    advantage at (N−1)/R — the doors you did not pick, divided by the doors still
    closed. Adding doors without adding reveals raises both together. In this model
    the quantity that moves the answer is <strong>how much the host reveals</strong>,
    not how many doors there were to begin with.
  </p>

  <h2>Assumptions, and how the simulation is run</h2>

  <p>
    The formulas above hold under a specific set of rules, and every one of them
    matters. One prize is placed uniformly at random among N doors. The player
    picks one door. The host, who knows where the prize is, then opens K doors,
    never the player's door and never the prize. The player may move to any one of
    the R doors still closed, chosen at random rather than by a hunch.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>The simulation.</strong> The Monte Carlo run does not evaluate the
    closed forms above, but it is not fully independent of them either: it skips
    the host entirely, drawing the switch target straight from the R survivors, so
    it assumes the same symmetry step the derivation uses. A separate check in the
    test file does build the doors, open them one at a time under two different host
    rules, and let the player choose — that run and this one agree. Randomness comes
    from a seeded generator rather than <code>Math.random</code>, so the seed in the
    URL reproduces the exact curve you are looking at.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Sources.</strong> Steve Selvin posed the problem in "A Problem in
    Probability", <a href="https://doi.org/10.1080/00031305.1975.10479121"><em>The
    American Statistician</em> 29(1), February 1975, p. 67</a> — with three boxes,
    not doors. He returned to it in "On the Monty Hall Problem",
    <a href="https://doi.org/10.1080/00031305.1975.10477398">29(3), August 1975,
    p. 134</a>, the earliest known appearance of that name in print. The N-door
    generalisation follows from the same argument; it is commonly credited to a 1975
    letter to Selvin from D. L. Ferguson, an attribution reported here rather than
    verified — the original letter could not be consulted. The problem reached a
    wide audience through Marilyn vos Savant's "Ask Marilyn" column in
    <em>Parade</em>, 9 September 1990; the letter counts above are from
    <a href="https://www2.math.upenn.edu/~kazdan/210/notes-misc/Monty_Hall.html">John
    Tierney's report in <em>The New York Times</em>, 21 July 1991</a>. The Erdős
    episode is
    <a href="https://sites.oxy.edu/lengyel/M372/Vazsonyi2003/vazs30_1.pdf">Andrew
    Vazsonyi's own account</a>. Jason Rosenhouse's
    <a href="https://doi.org/10.1093/oso/9780195367898.001.0001"><em>The Monty Hall
    Problem</em></a> (Oxford University Press, 2009) covers the variants.
  </p>

  <h2>One case, worked by hand</h2>

  <p>Take N=4 and K=1, a case where the answer is neither 1/2 nor an obvious 1/N.</p>

  <p>
    Four doors, one prize. You pick door 1. Staying wins whenever the prize is
    behind door 1, which happens in 1 of 4 games: <strong>25.0%</strong>.
  </p>

  <p>
    Now the other branch. In 3 of those 4 games your pick is wrong and the prize is
    behind door 2, 3 or 4. The host opens one losing door from that group. Since the
    host never opens the prize door, two doors are left for you to move to, and the
    prize is behind exactly one of them. Given a wrong first pick you switch into
    the prize half the time: <code>(3/4) × (1/2) = 3/8 = 37.5%</code>.
  </p>

  <p>
    In this model switching sits above staying — 37.5% against 25.0% — but 37.5% is
    a long way from the 66.7% that the three-door version trains people to expect.
    The remaining 37.5% of games are the ones where you switch to the wrong door of
    the two: 25.0 + 37.5 + 37.5 = 100. Set the sliders to N=4, K=1 and the summary
    line under the chart states those exact figures; the two win-rate cards show the
    simulated averages, which land near them once enough games have run.
  </p>

  <h2>What this model does not cover</h2>

  <p>
    The result depends entirely on the host's rule, and this model fixes that rule:
    the host always knows where the prize is, always opens losing doors, never opens
    the player's door, and always offers the switch. Change any one of those and the
    numbers change. A model in which the host opens doors at random, restricted to
    the games where the prize happened to stay hidden, puts switching and staying
    level. A model in which the switch is offered only when the player is already on
    the prize puts switching at zero. Both look identical from the player's seat.
  </p>

  <p>
    The model also assumes the prize is placed uniformly and that the switch target
    is chosen at random rather than on a hunch. That last assumption does more work
    than it appears to. A host with a habit — always opening the lowest-numbered
    losing door, say — leaks information through <em>which</em> doors were opened. A
    model that includes such a host, and a player who reasons from the habit, returns
    a higher number than this one whenever more than one door is left to switch into:
    at four doors and one reveal it gives 50% where this model gives 37.5%. Once the
    host opens every door but one, the two models agree — with a single door left
    there is nothing to choose, and the leak can never make staying the better bet.
  </p>

  <p>
    Sampling noise sets a hard limit on what the curve can show, and at this page's
    headline setting it bites. With a hundred doors and one reveal the exact gap is
    0.01 percentage points, while the standard error at a million trials — the most
    the slider offers — is about the same size. The dashed exact lines carry that
    result; the solid simulated ones cannot resolve it at any setting available
    here, and the chart says so in its caption when the two lines fall closer
    together than the width of the ink.
  </p>

  <p>
    One further limit is structural: the simulation never represents <em>which</em>
    doors the host opened, only how many survive. Any variant that turns on which door was
    opened — the biased host above among them — is outside what this widget can
    compute, and the figures quoted for it come from working through every case by
    hand rather than from the sliders. The generator is a seeded PRNG for
    reproducibility, not a cryptographic source.
  </p>

